  <ProjectNotes>There are over 200 remote microgrids throughout Alaska. Thanks in large part to the efforts of these communities and energy advocates around the state, about 35 of these have been converterd to renewable-hybrid microgrids, mostly based on wind. Beginning with our origins at the National Renewable Energy Laboratory, HOMER Energy has partnered with local energy experts for over two decades to provide a standardized evaluation to justify investments in wind to improve the energy supplies in these communities.

This model represents a hypothetical Alaskan remote community that has been retrofitted with wind in a fuel-saver like application. Under this approach, there is limited need for storage and the primary benefit of the wind is to reduce the need for importing for importing expensive diesel. This improves the resiliency of the community, and reduces the burden of getting fuel to the communities--particularly in consideration of the harsh environmental and limited transportation infrasctructure.

In this model, I performed a sensitiviy on the diesel fuel price and the wind speed price. By selecting the "Graphical" radio button i nthe "Results" View you can see the wind speed and diesel price at which wind is cost-effective vs. not.</ProjectNotes>
  <ProjectTitle>Wind-Diesel Remote Microgrid</ProjectTitle>
